//
// Probe: `save`/`load` and their families, `bootstrap`, `save.script`,
// `load.script` and `alias=` — `bund/bund`'s world words.
//
// **D27: redb where the reference uses SQLite**, with the same wire bytes
// (D20). The five tables mirror the reference's: `ALIASES`, `LAMBDAS`,
// `STACKS` with `STACK_DATA`, and `BOOTSTRAP`.
//
// **The sequence below is the one F148 records, and its order is forced.**
// `save.script` cannot create the bootstrap table — only `save` does — so a
// script can only be stored *after* a `save`; and `save` empties that table,
// so a second `save` destroys every script. A program cannot keep its world
// and its scripts both current, and this probe pins that rather than leaving
// it to be met twice.
//
// The world file lands in the working directory whatever name is given,
// because the name is cleaned as the reference cleans it — path separators
// removed, `.world` appended. **So the probe removes it before and after
// itself**, as `models-world.bund` does: each engine writes its own kind of
// file (SQLite against redb, D27), and a capture runs each program twice.
//
// Two consecutive runs agree even with a stale file, because every `save`
// replaces its tables wholesale rather than merging — checked, because a
// probe whose second run differs is one the capture refuses.
//
"bund2-probe-world.world" fs.rm drop
"probe: a lambda, an alias and two stacks, saved and loaded in one program" println
:sq { dup * } register
"println" "p" alias
@other 7 8
@main 1 2
"bund2-probe-world" save

"probe: load them back and use each one" println
"bund2-probe-world" load
"p" alias=
5 sq

"probe: save.script needs a prior save, then load.script returns the text" println
"s1" "100 200 +" "bund2-probe-world" save.script
"s1" "bund2-probe-world" load.script

"probe: bootstrap loads the world and runs every stored script" println
"bund2-probe-world" bootstrap

"probe: load appends, so @other now holds its values three times over" println
"bund2-probe-world.world" fs.rm drop
